(3195588, 444719, 29797, '<td valign="top">I''m suprised as well this ended up where it did. Nice vintage feel to the photo with the processing. One thing that I see is you have 2 subjects here that compete with each other. Given the title, I would say the lighthouse is the subject, but that tree is so overpowering and interesting that I want to look at that more than the lighthouse, but <i>then</i> I go back to the lighthouse and I don''t know where to finally settle my eyes on. <br/> <br/>Granted, I don''t know the location, but maybe a few steps back and a couple to the left would make full use out of this interesting composition and full frame aspect ratio, so that the tree isn''t so tall and the lighthouse is more prominent. I hope that makes sense. As it is, maybe a crop to a 1.25/1 aspect to get rid of some of that empty space on the left would make it a little more appealing to some.<br/> <br/>Edit: just noticed how long my comment was, sorry if I babbled there.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-01-08 11:01:21.</i></td>', 'I''m suprised as well this ended up where it did. Nice vintage feel to the photo with the processing. One thing that I see is you have 2 subjects here that compete with each other. Given the title, I would say the lighthouse is the subject, but that tree is so overpowering and interesting that I want to look at that more than the lighthouse, but then I go back to the lighthouse and I don''t know where to finally settle my eyes on.  Granted, I don''t know the location, but maybe a few steps back and a couple to the left would make full use out of this interesting composition and full frame aspect ratio, so that the tree isn''t so tall and the lighthouse is more prominent. I hope that makes sense. As it is, maybe a crop to a 1.25/1 aspect to get rid of some of that empty space on the left would make it a little more appealing to some. Edit: just noticed how long my comment was, sorry if I babbled there.', '2007-01-08 11:00:20.000000', '2007-01-08 11:01:21.000000', 0),
(3195590, 440862, 60862, '<td valign="top">This is the best depth of field in your three kitty shots so far.  I very much like having almost the entire face in focus.  Combine this DOF with the pose from "Depressed?" and I think you''ll break 6.0.  Also think about the lighting.  Her pupils are the widest here out of the three shots, and they look like vast pools you could fall into.  It might also be interesting to get her looking into a bright light so you get the classic cat eye slit.<br/> <br/>Unfortunately, outside of pet challenges, I think many people just don''t care for this kind of shot.</td>', 'This is the best depth of field in your three kitty shots so far.  I very much like having almost the entire face in focus.  Combine this DOF with the pose from "Depressed?" and I think you''ll break 6.0.  Also think about the lighting.  Her pupils are the widest here out of the three shots, and they look like vast pools you could fall into.  It might also be interesting to get her looking into a bright light so you get the classic cat eye slit. Unfortunately, outside of pet challenges, I think many people just don''t care for this kind of shot.', '2007-01-08 11:00:50.000000', NULL, 0),
