(3209954, 441813, 66384, '<td valign="top">This got an 8 from me. I know how hard it is to capture a really good shot of the white egrets. They hold nicely still, but the detail is hard to get in the bright light. Here the highlights are blown a little on the back and head. I wonder if Highlights/Shadows together with Exposure might restore a little of the detail in those areas. Also I wish I could see the rest of the plumage in the back. The crop looks a little tight there. I''m guessing there may have been some visual obstruction that you had to crop out.<br/> <br/>While <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=13958" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">RUEDISCHMUTZ</a>''s "golden rule" is good, I don''t think it applies here, because to me the blowing plumage is an interesting element, and a very tight crop would make a very ordinary shot. This was a good entry, and was well appreciated by the voters.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-01-10 23:41:47.</i></td>', 'This got an 8 from me. I know how hard it is to capture a really good shot of the white egrets. They hold nicely still, but the detail is hard to get in the bright light. Here the highlights are blown a little on the back and head. I wonder if Highlights/Shadows together with Exposure might restore a little of the detail in those areas. Also I wish I could see the rest of the plumage in the back. The crop looks a little tight there. I''m guessing there may have been some visual obstruction that you had to crop out. While  RUEDISCHMUTZ''s "golden rule" is good, I don''t think it applies here, because to me the blowing plumage is an interesting element, and a very tight crop would make a very ordinary shot. This was a good entry, and was well appreciated by the voters.', '2007-01-10 23:39:10.000000', '2007-01-10 23:41:47.000000', 0),
(3210047, 435224, 66384, '<td valign="top">Bob, sorry I didn''t leave a comment during voting -- too many entries. I like woods, and I gave this shot a 7. I like the patches of sunlight on the ground snow. It makes the place friendly and non-threatening. Something about the processing has given the image a very gray appearance, and I would prefer to see the natural look of snow.</td>', 'Bob, sorry I didn''t leave a comment during voting -- too many entries. I like woods, and I gave this shot a 7. I like the patches of sunlight on the ground snow. It makes the place friendly and non-threatening. Something about the processing has given the image a very gray appearance, and I would prefer to see the natural look of snow.', '2007-01-10 23:54:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(3210132, 444354, 69008, '<td valign="top">Just using a polarizer would have made the colors much richer, the sky darker, and the haze much less in this image.<br/> You may have tripped the shutter just a little too soon. I feel that the image would have more interest if more of the side of the engine was in it. <br/> As you indicate, it''s a "grab" shot, and not a thought out set up. <br/>Flat black things like the front of the boiler are very tricky to expose so that you get any detail, because they suck up so much light that you have to overexpose everything else to get good detail in the black. <br/>I see that many of the other comments key in on post processing, but the best thing is to get the image near right to start with when the shutter clicks. That takes experience, and will come if you keep using the camera, and thinking about how to best capture the moment that you want. I hope that I do not irritate you with honest comments.  Keep clicking.</td>', 'Just using a polarizer would have made the colors much richer, the sky darker, and the haze much less in this image. You may have tripped the shutter just a little too soon. I feel that the image would have more interest if more of the side of the engine was in it.  As you indicate, it''s a "grab" shot, and not a thought out set up. Flat black things like the front of the boiler are very tricky to expose so that you get any detail, because they suck up so much light that you have to overexpose everything else to get good detail in the black. I see that many of the other comments key in on post processing, but the best thing is to get the image near right to start with when the shutter clicks. That takes experience, and will come if you keep using the camera, and thinking about how to best capture the moment that you want. I hope that I do not irritate you with honest comments.  Keep clicking.', '2007-01-11 00:08:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(3210200, 444354, 69572, '<td valign="top">MelonMusketeer<br/> <br/>You in no way irritate me with your comments.  They are very handy to read.  Like my website (located in my profile) says I''m a beginner that wishes to learn.  I have some equipment with the entry level Pentax *IST DL Entry-Level DSLR.  Please comment on my others if you like.  I wish to learn as much as possible.  Also the tutorials on this site are very good and I''m learning quite a lot.  Short term goal is to have a picture that gets a score above six.  My procrastination shot is currently at 5.3.  I'' very happy with that one.</td>', 'MelonMusketeer You in no way irritate me with your comments.  They are very handy to read.  Like my website (located in my profile) says I''m a beginner that wishes to learn.  I have some equipment with the entry level Pentax *IST DL Entry-Level DSLR.  Please comment on my others if you like.  I wish to learn as much as possible.  Also the tutorials on this site are very good and I''m learning quite a lot.  Short term goal is to have a picture that gets a score above six.  My procrastination shot is currently at 5.3.  I'' very happy with that one.', '2007-01-11 00:21:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(3210726, 444387, 68546, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the comments, it''s hard to pick a single shot from over a month but i just liked the detail and composition of this one. I''m glad you liked it</td>', 'Thanks for the comments, it''s hard to pick a single shot from over a month but i just liked the detail and composition of this one. I''m glad you liked it', '2007-01-11 03:48:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(3211291, 441110, 70107, '<td valign="top">it looks beautiful.. and theres such a lovely contrast with human and nature that you catch this photo with such authenticity and beauty.</td>', 'it looks beautiful.. and theres such a lovely contrast with human and nature that you catch this photo with such authenticity and beauty.', '2007-01-11 08:55:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(3211311, 443640, 6870, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by agenkin:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by agenkin:</b></div><hr/><i>How did you get the clouds so blurry at 1/80s shutter?  I think that I''m seeing motion blur from PP, but I may be wrong.</i></td></tr></table> <br/>Can you respond to this, please? :) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>The clouds are blurry from a combo of NeatImage, median, and gaussian blur and various degrees of sharpening after each one. All the blurring tools were used at very low "power." I had to have the sky on a different layer so I could work on it. I also used Unsharp Mask at a large pixel radius and low percentage to add contrast and bring out more texture. I converted to b/w using the channel mixer, which of course created some noise, so these various tools were used to simply rid the sky of noise and enhance the lacy texture of the clouds.</td>', 'Originally posted by agenkin: Originally posted by agenkin:How did you get the clouds so blurry at 1/80s shutter?  I think that I''m seeing motion blur from PP, but I may be wrong. Can you respond to this, please? :)   The clouds are blurry from a combo of NeatImage, median, and gaussian blur and various degrees of sharpening after each one. All the blurring tools were used at very low "power." I had to have the sky on a different layer so I could work on it. I also used Unsharp Mask at a large pixel radius and low percentage to add contrast and bring out more texture. I converted to b/w using the channel mixer, which of course created some noise, so these various tools were used to simply rid the sky of noise and enhance the lacy texture of the clouds.', '2007-01-11 09:02:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(3212191, 443640, 66310, '<td valign="top">gorgeous! Nicely done!</td>', 'gorgeous! Nicely done!', '2007-01-11 12:32:29.000000', NULL, 0),
